00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.500
SARA JAMES: Hello my
name is Sara James.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:16.083
I've been working
the past seven years

00:00:16.083 --> 00:00:19.510
at MIT Lincoln Laboratory,
doing radar signal processing.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:21.980
Today, I'm here to tell you
about the image processing

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.620
workshop of the girls who build.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.040
So the image processing
section of this workshop

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.420
is actually done with a
software called Processing.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:31.590
Processing is open
source, free, and it's

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.760
available at Processing.org
to download, for just

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.260
about any sort of operating
system that you can think of.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.718
This software is
actually developed

00:00:38.718 --> 00:00:41.150
for people that are not
doing coding, day to day.

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:42.660
During this section
of the workshop

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.510
we're trying to
expose participants

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:47.440
to image processing
concepts, within the context

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.250
of real world applications.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:53.060
Furthermore, we're trying to
demystify coding, and show

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.700
participants that
they can do this too.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.770
You'll be doing four exercises.

00:00:57.770 --> 00:00:59.870
The first being an
Instagram filter.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:01.360
For the Instagram
filter this will

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.680
be adding an overlay where
you can change the RGB values.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.880
Next you'll be
flipping an image.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.890
This will be a flip
that's vertical,

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:11.800
and during this you'll
be actually moving

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.660
around the individual pixels.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.600
After this, you will
convert a color image

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.750
to a different color scheme.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:22.720
So you'll take something, and
convert it to one single color.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.970
This involves modifying
the individual color values

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:27.950
for every pixel.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:30.550
For the last exercise,
which is the vignette,

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:32.640
this is adding a
border to the picture.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.040
The way that this is performed,
is by doing a mask overlay.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.640
Before you just jump into
having the girls doing

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.540
the fill in the blank exercises,
it's highly recommended

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.880
to go over the four processing
components of any processing

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:48.470
program.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.550
The first is
variable Declaration:

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:53.360
this is where you
initialize something

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.860
as being a PImage or
perhaps a Boolean,

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.900
depending on the exercise.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.370
Next is the setup.

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:02.340
The setup is where you
generally load in images

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.200
and initialize the various
settings for a program.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.672
After that you'll have a Draw.

00:02:08.672 --> 00:02:10.880
Draw is where you
actually produce the frame

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.030
that you'll see.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:17.050
Lastly you'll have, in some of
the exercises, Mouseclicked.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:20.910
And this is to handle
mouse click events.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.060
Before breaking the
girls up into groups,

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.640
we had a image
processing lecture,

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.010
that was specifically tailored
towards the processing

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:28.990
language.

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:31.830
During this lecture,
various fundamentals

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:33.360
of image processing
were introduced

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.370
like color schemes--
for instance RGB

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:40.120
or things like HSBC-- in
discussing brightness.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:42.800
After the processing lecture,
we broke up the girls

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:46.710
into groups of four, with
one volunteer per group.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:48.290
When the girls were
broken up, they

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:49.950
were given their exercises.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:53.430
These exercises were set up as
fill in the blank activities.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.100
The fill in the
blank style was done

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.960
to level the playing
field, so that people

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.920
with more or less
experience with coding

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:02.080
would feel comfortable.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:03.980
Time permitting--
and also dependent

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.450
on the experience
level of the girls

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.430
that you're working with--
there's additional activities

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:12.000
that you can do, within the
context of the four coding

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.310
exercises that have
already been defined.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:18.310
For the first one, for your
build your own Instagram

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:21.720
filter, you can actually
modify the RGB values

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.590
to change the overlay.

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:25.924
So this is something that's
relatively easy to do,

00:03:25.924 --> 00:03:27.590
and something that
you should definitely

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:29.420
encourage the girls to do.

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:32.670
For the flip an image, you
can actually change the way

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:33.580
that the images flip.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.640
So in the exercises
that we had, the image

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.915
is flipped vertically,
but a more difficult thing

00:03:40.915 --> 00:03:42.290
that you can ask
the girls to do,

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:44.790
is to flip the
image horizontally.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.800
So within the convert an
image to a single color,

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:50.240
you can actually ask the
girls to manually convert

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.040
to grayscale.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:53.870
For this activity,
this is something

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:58.240
that's introduce the girls to
the way that color is perceived

00:03:58.240 --> 00:03:59.290
by humans.

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:03.666
For the add a vignette,
one relatively easy thing

00:04:03.666 --> 00:04:05.040
that you can ask
the girls to do,

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.420
is vary the size and
color of the vignette.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:11.270
Lastly, and this is
the most difficult,

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:13.460
you can actually
ask the participants

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:17.240
to combine all elements,
from all of the exercises.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.670
This is something that
is the most complex,

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:22.451
because it involves
potentially changing

00:04:22.451 --> 00:04:23.950
the way that the
code was originally

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:25.920
run in the exercises.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.724
So in terms of the
lessons learned,

00:04:27.724 --> 00:04:29.390
there are certain
things that we did not

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:32.300
do, during girls who
build, and in hindsight we

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:35.230
would have done, if we
were to do this again.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:36.950
One of the first
things is actually

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:39.930
introducing the
fundamentals of programming.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.390
Things like introducing
if statements,

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:46.640
while statements, what a
Boolean is, things like that.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.710
When we had done girls
who build, we kind of just

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:52.020
jumped right into the exercises,
after an image processing

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.270
lecture.

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:57.470
And that wasn't really enough
preparation for the girls.

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:00.506
The first exercise was very
difficult to get through.

00:05:00.506 --> 00:05:02.630
That's another thing that
you could potentially do.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:05.580
If you have people that are not
really experienced with coding,

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.340
feel free to go through
the first exercise

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:10.170
together, and then have the
students do the following three

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:12.320
exercises individually.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:15.910
Each individual student
had their own laptop,

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:18.700
don't think that this is
something that you need to do.

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:22.420
You may find that having
multiple students with only one

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:25.800
laptop will be sufficient, and
actually might introduce them

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.280
to working in a group, and
the type of collaboration that

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.110
entails.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:32.470
Also along those lines.

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:33.950
If everyone has
their own computer,

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:36.160
you might find that
it's very isolating.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.610
One thing that girls
who build did not do,

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.120
but it's something that we
may look into in the future,

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:44.190
is using some sort of
collaborative editing software.

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:47.097
An example of this is TitanPad.

00:05:47.097 --> 00:05:48.680
Whether you're a
student or a teacher,

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:51.780
you may be asking yourself,
what are the next steps?

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:53.690
The next steps could
possibly be looking

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:55.670
into other coding languages.

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:59.220
Coding languages that you may
want to look into include c++,

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.050
Java, or Python.

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:03.290
You'll find that, for
instance, if you wanted

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:05.430
to build an Android
app, there's communities

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:08.110
and resources available
online for free.

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:09.317
That you can access.

00:06:09.317 --> 00:06:11.150
Have fun with the image
processing activity!

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:12.816
We'd love to hear
about your experience.

00:06:12.816 --> 00:06:17.750
Email:

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:18.950
Girls Who Build.

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:20.200
Girlswhobuild.org

